    "rawOutput": {
      "result": {
        "success": {
          "content": "{\n  \"mode\": \"single_tool\",\n  \"server\": \"cursor-cloud\",\n  \"serverStatus\": \"ready\",\n  \"serverDescription\": \"Cursor-internal cloud-agent diagnostics (server `cursor-cloud`). - `cursor-cloud-run-info` — return the current cloud-agent run identity, basic run metadata (name, repo, branch, model, owners — including the owning user's name and email when resolvable, lifecycle and setup status, created-at), and the URL where the run is available (https://cursor.com/agents/<bcId>). - `cursor-cloud-list-cloud-agents` — list cloud agents accessible to this run's principal in THIS run's environment (or, when this run has no saved environment, agents on the same repository), ordered by creation time (newest first), returning basic run metadata plus URLs. Users and service accounts see their own runs. Team admins can additionally browse teammates' runs only when enabled in team MCP settings. Supports browse filters (source, lifecycle status, setup status, recency/creation windows, archived, self-hosted, whether the agent made code changes, and whether it created a pull request) plus limit/offset paging. When counting or enumerating many runs, page with nextOffset until hasMore is false; use totalMatched only when the accessible scan is complete. - `cursor-cloud-environment-info` — return THIS run's environment (name, environment & version public ids, source, recorded-via/created-at provenance, repos, full environment.json, dashboard URL, and environmentDeleted when the linked environment is soft-deleted), a top-level `repos` list (same canonical URLs as environment.repos / trigger-environment-build refs[].repoUrl; still populated when environment is null from composer.repoUrl), effective egress policy (allow-all, the complete merged allowed-domain list, or unresolved with a reason), the environment build the pod booted from (build id, snapshot id, git-setup mode, warm-fork mode) or null when the run started just-in-time rather than from a build, and (for internal runs) the initiating Cursor client build info (version + commit SHA, same as Help → About). - `cursor-cloud-get-events` — list the events recorded for THIS run (the same events shown on the run's dashboard page), oldest first: run lifecycle and setup outcomes plus soft failures such as MCP authentication errors (kind `mcp_auth_error` — an MCP server rejected this run's credentials, so its tool calls failed while the run continued). Each event carries publicId, createdAtMs, category, logLevel, a stable kind, a user-facing title, and optional detail and linkUrl. - `cursor-cloud-get-message-queue` — return a point-in-time snapshot of THIS run's user-visible pending follow-up queue (the same queue shown on the dashboard). No arguments; always this run. The currently executing message is no longer queued. Defer slow testing/verification while queued messages remain, then verify the cumulative result when the queue is empty. Returns hasQueuedMessages, queuedMessageCount, and queued message metadata (followupId, createdAtMs, source, text). Does not include attachments or internal queue entries. - `cursor-cloud-batch-fetch-details` — fetch details for a batch of specified accessible cloud agents by bcIds. Users and service accounts can fetch their own runs. Team admins can additionally fetch teammates' runs only when enabled in team MCP settings. When matching agents are found, writes basic run info to index.json and optionally writes diff-metadata.json, setup-logs.txt, start-logs.txt, environment-info.json, events.json, and transcript.json under /tmp/cursor/cloud-agent-transcripts/<datetime-id>/<bcId>/. Set includeEvents to include each run's dashboard events. First-class Task results include cloudAgentBcId; pass those ids to another batch-fetch-details call to fetch child transcripts. Transcripts can be very large — read them via subagents, never directly. - `cursor-cloud-get-automation` — look up one Cursor automation by id (UUID) via the AutomationsService GetAutomation API, as the run's principal: returns automation metadata (id, name, enabled state, display owner/creator, owner email, and URL) when readable, the same metadata fields for a teammate's private automation when only a restricted summary is visible, or a not-found error otherwise. - `cursor-cloud-list-environment-builds` — list this run's environment builds (the prebuilt environment snapshots a cloud agent can boot from), newest first, with status/failure outcomes. Returns an empty list when the run has no linked environment. Supports statuses and createdAfter/createdBefore filters plus limit; page older builds by passing nextCursor back as cursor. - `cursor-cloud-environment-build-logs` — fetch the install/setup logs for one environment build by buildId (returned inline; large outputs are spilled to a file automatically). Build logs are retained ~10 days; only builds of this run's own environment are accessible. - `cursor-cloud-trigger-environment-build` — start one DRAFT environment build of this run's own environment and return its buildId. OK to call during environment setup to validate the config before proposing, or when the user asks to trigger a build. Each build boots a pod and runs the full install, so skip it outside those cases. Supports per-repo ref overrides (`refs`) and a custom `environmentJson` config override for testing config changes. For a greenfield run with no linked environment, pass the complete `environmentJson`; the trigger creates and links a personal transitional draft automatically. Draft builds never become the build new agents boot from; track them with list-environment-builds and fetch logs with environment-build-logs. - `cursor-cloud-request-environment-setup-actions` — record one or more user actions that currently block environment setup. Calls may be made more than once in a turn; successful calls from the completed turn are combined into one request for the user. Presenting results: unless the user asks for a specific format or a summary, show a tool call's returned result to the user verbatim as pretty-printed JSON in a `json` code block rather than paraphrasing or summarizing it. This applies only to data returned directly by the tool call, not to detail files written to the filesystem (e.g. batch-fetch-details output).\",\n  \"tool\": {\n    \"tool\": \"get-message-queue\",\n    \"description\": \"Return a point-in-time snapshot of THIS run's user-visible pending follow-up message queue — the same queue shown on the dashboard. No arguments; always scoped to this run. The currently executing message is no longer queued. Defer slow testing and verification while queued messages remain, then verify the cumulative result when the queue is empty. Returns hasQueuedMessages, queuedMessageCount, and queued message metadata (followupId, createdAtMs, source, text). Does not include attachments or internal queue entries.\",\n    \"inputSchema\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\",\n      \"properties\": {},\n      \"additionalProperties\": false,\n      \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#\"\n    }\n  }\n}"
        }
      }
    }
